WEBVTT - Most Fascinating Stories - Episode 259: Edgar Allan Poe: The Master of the Macabre, 'The Raven', and the Mystery at Baltimore

1
00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:05.680
[Rebecca] Welcome to Most Fascinating Stories.

2
00:00:05.680 --> 00:00:06.880
[Rebecca] I'm Rebecca.

3
00:00:06.880 --> 00:00:13.760
[Rebecca] Today, our top 50 famous non-president American series explores the haunted shadows of the human mind.

4
00:00:13.760 --> 00:00:20.240
[Rebecca] The orphaned poet and critic who invented detective fiction and elevated Gothic horror into high art.

5
00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:27.520
[Rebecca] Here is George with the tragic, incandescent life of Edgar Allan Poe.

6
00:00:27.520 --> 00:00:33.520
[George] Born in Boston in January 1809 to traveling stage actors David Poe J.R.

7
00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:40.240
[George] and Elizabeth Arnold Hopkins, Edgar Poe was born into a world of theatrical glamour and crushing poverty.

8
00:00:40.240 --> 00:00:54.000
[George] When his father abandoned the family and his beautiful 24-year-old mother coughed her life away from tuberculosis in a Richmond boarding house in December 1811, two-year-old Edgar was left an orphan pauper beside her corpse.

9
00:00:54.000 --> 00:01:04.880
[George] He was taken into the home of John Allen, a wealthy, stern Scottish merchant in Richmond, Virginia, who gave Edgar his middle name Alan but never legally adopted him.

10
00:01:04.880 --> 00:01:17.360
[George] Edgar grew up in privileged comfort, accompanying the family to England, where he studied Latin and French at Stoke Newington, developing a romantic imagination steeped in medieval tombs and gothic architecture.

11
00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:27.040
[George] But as Edgar matured into an introspective, rebellious young poet, his relationship with his conservative foster father fractured over money and morality.

12
00:01:27.040 --> 00:01:34.240
[George] Entering the newly founded University of Virginia in 1826, Edgar excelled in languages and literature.

13
00:01:45.560 --> 00:01:55.320
[George] When Alan coldly refused to pay the debts, Edgar was forced to flee the university, burning his dormitory furniture to keep warm during his final nights.

14
00:01:55.320 --> 00:02:11.400
[George] Young Edgar's early childhood in the theatrical boarding houses of Boston and Richmond exposed him to the dramatic flair of Shakespearean tragedy, an atmosphere of romantic artifice that shaped his lifelong fascination with mortality, beauty, and the theatrical mask.

15
00:02:11.400 --> 00:02:23.960
[George] His foster mother Frances Allen lavished him with affectionate tenderness, shielding the sensitive boy from John Allen's stern, mercantile disapproval until her tragic death shattered Edgar's domestic refuge.

16
00:02:23.960 --> 00:02:35.080
[George] Penniless and estranged from his foster father, 18-year-old Poe fled to Boston in 1827, enlisting in the United States Army as a private under the pseudonym Edgar A.

17
00:02:35.080 --> 00:02:35.960
[George] Perry.

18
00:02:35.960 --> 00:02:51.160
[George] Serving in the artillery at Fort Independence and Fort Moultrie on Sullivan's Island in South Carolina, where the desolate barrier beaches later inspired his famous cryptographic treasure story, The Gold Bug, Poe proved to be an exemplary soldier.

19
00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:56.280
[George] Rising to the rank of sergeant major, the highest non-commissioned rank possible.

20
00:02:56.280 --> 00:03:04.920
[George] That same year, he published his first anonymous poetry collection, Tamerlane and Other Poems, bearing the credit by a Bostonian.

21
00:03:04.920 --> 00:03:13.960
[George] Seeking a military commission to appease John Allen, Poe secured an appointment to the United States Military Academy at West Point in 1830.

22
00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:22.680
[George] However, when Alan remarried without informing Edgar and cut off all future inheritance, Poe recognized that a military career was futile.

23
00:03:22.680 --> 00:03:40.600
[George] Deliberately neglecting his roll calls, chapel attendance, and military drills, Poe courted a court martial, being expelled from West Point in February 1831, but not before his fellow cadets pooled 75 cents each to fund the publication of his third volume of poetry.

24
00:03:40.600 --> 00:03:43.720
[George] Dedicated affectionately to the Corps of Cadets.

25
00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:59.360
[George] At Sullivan's Island in South Carolina, Poe spent long hours exploring the windswept barrier sand dunes and maritime myrtle thickets, observing the flight of herons and collecting sea shells that later featured in his pioneering cryptographic and mystery tales.

26
00:03:59.360 --> 00:04:10.480
[George] His expulsion from West Point was a calculated act of artistic independence, demonstrating his refusal to surrender his poetic vocation to the rigid discipline of military engineering.

27
00:04:10.480 --> 00:04:18.960
[George] Arriving in Baltimore, Poe found refuge in the humble household of his widowed aunt Maria Clemm and his young cousin Virginia Clem.

28
00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:31.040
[George] Struggling against absolute destitution, Poe began writing prose fiction, winning a $50 prize in 1833 from the Baltimore Saturday Visitor for MS, found in a bottle.

29
00:04:31.040 --> 00:04:46.480
[George] In 1835, Poe became editor of the Southern Literary Messenger in Richmond, where his razor-sharp, uncompromising literary criticism earned him the feared nickname, The Tomahawk Man, tripling the magazine's circulation within a year.

30
00:04:46.480 --> 00:04:54.480
[George] In May 1836, 27-year-old Poe married his cousin Virginia Clem, who was not yet fourteen years old.

31
00:04:54.480 --> 00:05:03.440
[George] While 19th-century observers found the marriage unusual, Virginia was Poe's cherished idol, beloved sister, and ethereal muse.

32
00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:17.120
[George] Together with Maria Clem, whom Poe affectionately called Muddy, they formed a fiercely loyal, affectionate domestic trinity that sustained Poe through bouts of crippling depression, poverty, and sporadic struggles with alcohol.

33
00:05:17.120 --> 00:05:32.360
[George] In Richmond at the Southern Literary Messenger, Poe's fearless book reviews attacked the incestuous Mutual Admiration Society of Northern Publishers, insisting upon rigorous aesthetic standards and establishing modern American literary criticism.

34
00:05:32.360 --> 00:05:46.120
[George] His deep emotional devotion to Virginia and Maria Clemm created a fragile sanctuary of mutual love, where despite unpaid bills and freezing winters, the young family shared warmth, laughter, and poetic dreams.

35
00:05:46.120 --> 00:06:03.080
[George] In April 1841, while serving as editor of Graham's magazine in Philadelphia, Poe published a revolutionary story that founded an entire global genre of literature the murders in the Rue Morgue, introducing the brilliant, eccentric Parisian gentleman C.

36
00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:19.000
[George] Auguste Dupin, who solved the horrific locked room murder of two women through rattyosination, the rigorous, scientific application of deductive logic, psychological observation, and lateral thinking, Poe invented the modern detective story.

37
00:06:19.000 --> 00:06:31.160
[George] Dupin's analytical brilliance, pipe-smoking contemplation, and narration by an admiring companion established the exact narrative formula that Sir Arthur Conan Doyle later modeled for Sherlock Holmes and Dr.

38
00:06:31.160 --> 00:06:34.600
[George] Watson, and Agatha Christie for Hercule Poirot.

39
00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:41.480
[George] Conan Doyle later confessed each of Poe's detective stories is a route from which a whole literature has developed.

40
00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:45.640
[George] Where was the detective story until Poe breathed the breath of life into it?

41
00:06:45.640 --> 00:06:47.720
[George] The analytical brilliance of C.

42
00:06:48.120 --> 00:06:59.800
[George] August Dupin reflected Poe's conviction that the human intellect could decipher the most baffling enigmas of the universe through disciplined observation, mathematical logic, and imaginative empathy.

43
00:06:59.800 --> 00:07:12.440
[George] By introducing the eccentric, reclusive detective and his loyal chronicler, Poe created the universal template for detective fiction that continues to dominate world literature, television, and cinema.

44
00:07:12.440 --> 00:07:26.000
[George] In January 1842, while Virginia was singing at the piano in their cottage, she suddenly coughed up a bright red ribbon of blood, bursting a blood vessel in her throat, the fatal diagnosis of consumption.

45
00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:35.200
[George] For the next five agonizing years, Virginia lingered on the threshold of death, alternating between feverish recoveries and near fatal hemorrhages.

46
00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:41.600
[George] Poe was driven to the edge of madness by grief and terror, later writing, I became insane.

47
00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:44.480
[George] With long intervals of horrible sanity.

48
00:07:44.480 --> 00:07:58.160
[George] This intimate proximity to the creeping horror of tuberculosis fueled his greatest psychological masterpieces, the telltale heart, with its suffocating guilt and beating heart beneath the floorboards, the pit and the pendulum.

49
00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:12.320
[George] Exploring sensory deprivation and inquisition torture, the mask of the red death, depicting wealthy aristocrats sealing themselves in an abbey while a bloody pestilence stalks through locked iron gates, and the cask of a Montelado.

50
00:08:12.320 --> 00:08:18.400
[George] An icy study in vengeful psychopathy and walled entombment in subterranean catacombs.

51
00:08:18.400 --> 00:08:30.800
[George] The slow, agonizing decline of Virginia from consumption drove Poe to the edge of emotional exhaustion, fueling the claustrophobic dread and psychological panic that permeate his greatest horror tales.

52
00:08:30.800 --> 00:08:40.240
[George] In the telltale Heart and the fall of the House of Usher, Poe bypassed supernatural monsters to explore the far more terrifying terrain of the human mind.

53
00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:45.600
[George] Guilt, paranoia, sensory madness, and the collapse of psychic identity.

54
00:08:45.600 --> 00:08:54.080
[George] On January 29, 1845, the New York Evening Mirror published Poe's poetic masterpiece, The Raven.

55
00:08:54.080 --> 00:09:08.760
[George] With its hypnotic trochaic octameter rhythm, eerie internal rhymes, and haunting refrain of Nevermore, croaked by an ebony bird perched upon a bust of Palace Athena, the poem exploded into an unprecedented international sensation.

56
00:09:08.760 --> 00:09:17.320
[George] Schoolchildren memorized its verses, adults recited it at evening salons, and Poe was mobbed by admirers in the streets of Manhattan.

57
00:09:17.320 --> 00:09:21.960
[George] Yet for this immortal work, Poe was paid approximately $1.

58
00:09:21.960 --> 00:09:33.160
[George] In 1846, seeking fresh country air for Virginia's failing lungs, Poe moved the family to a tiny white wooden cottage in the rural village of Fordham, in the Bronx.

59
00:09:33.160 --> 00:09:45.880
[George] There, on January 30, 1847, 24-year-old Virginia died on a straw mattress, wrapped in Poe's military overcoat with a tortoiseshell cat sleeping on her chest to keep her warm.

60
00:09:45.880 --> 00:09:58.520
[George] Heartbroken and shattered, Poe collapsed into a prolonged physical and emotional breakdown, walking the frozen hills of Fordham at night and pouring his sorrow into his final immortal lyric, Annable Lee.

61
00:09:58.520 --> 00:10:11.080
[George] The publication of The Raven was a cultural phenomenon that transformed Poe into the first American literary celebrity whose name was recognized by laborers, schoolteachers, and foreign intellectuals alike.

62
00:10:11.080 --> 00:10:25.800
[George] In the tiny Fordham cottage, surrounded by fruit orchards and country silence, Poe nursed Virginia with tender devotion, covering her frozen bed with his heavy military overcoat and holding her cold hands as she slipped away into the shadows.

63
00:10:25.800 --> 00:10:36.520
[George] In the autumn of 1849, Poe embarked on a southern lecture tour, reuniting in Richmond with his childhood sweetheart Elmira Royster Shelton and becoming engaged.

64
00:10:36.520 --> 00:10:42.040
[George] On September 27, Poe boarded a steamer north toward Philadelphia and New York.

65
00:10:42.040 --> 00:10:48.160
[George] What transpired over the next six days remains the greatest unsolved mystery in literary history.

66
00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:59.680
[George] On October 3, 1849, Poe was found lying semi-conscious in the gutter outside Ryan's fourth ward poles in Baltimore, a notorious polling tavern on election day.

67
00:10:59.680 --> 00:11:07.360
[George] He was dressed in cheap, dirty, ill-fitting secondhand clothes that were not his own, including a battered straw hat.

68
00:11:07.360 --> 00:11:18.560
[George] Rushed to Washington College Hospital, Poe spent four days drifting in and out of a raging delirium, shouting repeatedly into the empty air for a mysterious person named Reynolds.

69
00:11:18.560 --> 00:11:31.120
[George] In the early morning hours of Sunday, October 7, 1849, 40-year-old Edgar Allan Poe uttered his final whisper, Lord, help my poor soul, and ceased to breathe.

70
00:11:31.120 --> 00:11:45.440
[George] Theories for his death have ranged from cholera, rabies and brain fever to cooping, a violent 19th-century political practice where innocent victims were drugged, forced to vote repeatedly in disguise, and abandoned to die.

71
00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:59.840
[George] Yet through the shadows of his tragic life, Edgar Allan Poe created an immortal legacy that reshaped the world's imagination, proving that out of the deepest grief and terror can emerge the most exquisite, sublime beauty in human literature.

72
00:11:59.840 --> 00:12:12.480
[George] The tragic, mysterious circumstances surrounding Poe's death in Baltimore have fascinated scholars for generations, inspiring countless biographies, detective investigations, and gothic legends.

73
00:12:12.480 --> 00:12:22.480
[George] Despite the vicious post mortem slander published by his envious rival Rufus Wilmot Griswold, Edgar Allan Poe's reputation triumphed across the globe.

74
00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:33.080
[George] Translated by Charles Baudelaire, championed by French symbolists, and revered as the father of modern horror, mystery, and psychological fiction.

75
00:12:33.400 --> 00:12:36.680
[Rebecca] Thank you for listening to most fascinating stories.

76
00:12:36.680 --> 00:12:49.320
[Rebecca] Next week, our top 50 famous non-president American series continues with episode 260, Walt Whitman, Leaves of Grass, Song of Myself, The Civil War Hospitals, and Lincoln's Bar.

77
00:12:49.320 --> 00:12:51.800
[Rebecca] Until then, stay curious.

